  "image_name": "b73b78fce99fc41e292f664635d9d4c4.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes three common ways to place extracellular matrix (ECM) material onto a surface and shows how they create very different patterns. Overall, it contrasts a templated transfer method with two direct-deposition methods.\n\nAt the top, a microcontact printing process is illustrated. A raised “stamp” is coated with ECM, pressed onto a flat substrate, and then removed. This leaves behind a defined micropattern of ECM, shown as clean geometric stripes and small rectangular islands. The key message is precise transfer of ECM only where the stamp touches.\n\nThe lower left shows electrospinning. An ECM solution is fed through a spinneret under high voltage, producing a thin jet that whips and deposits as a tangled fibrous mat on the collector surface. This appears to mimic a more random, mesh-like architecture.\n\nThe lower right shows extrusion or direct writing through a nozzle. Here the ECM solution is dispensed in a controlled path to form parallel deposited lines on the substrate, creating an ordered printed pattern.\n\nTogether, the panels compare how stamping, electrospinning, and nozzle-based printing can organize ECM from sharply defined 2D motifs to disordered fiber networks and regular line tracks.",
